WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.530
Call options conveyed
to the buyer the right,

2
00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:19.320
but not the obligation to
purchase a set number of

3
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.800
shares of an underlying assets
at a predetermined price,

4
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.465
known as the strike price,

5
00:00:24.465 --> 00:00:28.560
prior to the time that the
option contract expires.

6
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.230
A call option buyer
does this with

7
00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.240
the expectation that the price

8
00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.190
of the underlying asset will rise

9
00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.815
at some point prior
to expiration.

10
00:00:37.815 --> 00:00:39.720
The buyer of the option pays

11
00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.210
a premium to the
seller for this right.

12
00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.470
For a call option,
the premium can be

13
00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:47.360
shown as a fixed cost
beneath the chart.

14
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.150
If the market price of

15
00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:52.150
the underlying asset rises
above the strike price,

16
00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:55.690
the cost of the premium is
offset by an increase in

17
00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:57.670
the option's value and the option

18
00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.580
may become increasingly
profitable.

19
00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.560
If not, an option,

20
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.395
buyer can lose the
entire premium.

21
00:01:05.395 --> 00:01:09.770
The option seller may have
unlimited risk of loss.

22
00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:12.010
Options involved risk and are

23
00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:14.185
not suitable for all investors.

24
00:01:14.185 --> 00:01:15.790
For more information, read

25
00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:19.520
the characteristics and risks
of standardized options.

26
00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:25.700
For a copy call 312-542-6901.

27
00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:29.245
Put options convey to
the buyer the right,

28
00:01:29.245 --> 00:01:31.870
but not the obligation
to sell a set number of

29
00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:35.275
shares of an underlying asset
at a predetermined price,

30
00:01:35.275 --> 00:01:37.105
the strike price prior to

31
00:01:37.105 --> 00:01:40.045
the time that they
option contract expires.

32
00:01:40.045 --> 00:01:42.370
A put option buyer does this with

33
00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:44.170
the expectation that the price of

34
00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.430
the underlying asset will

35
00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:48.265
fall at some point in the future.

36
00:01:48.265 --> 00:01:50.260
The buyer of the option pays

37
00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:52.955
a premium to the
seller for this right.

38
00:01:52.955 --> 00:01:55.570
For a put option, the
premium can be shown

39
00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:58.315
as a fixed cost
beneath the chart.

40
00:01:58.315 --> 00:02:00.220
As the market price of

41
00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.580
the underlying asset decreases
beneath the strike price,

42
00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:06.820
the cost of the premium is
offset by an increase in

43
00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:08.050
the option's value and

44
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:10.954
the option becomes
increasingly profitable.

45
00:02:10.954 --> 00:02:14.420
Options provide investors
with more leverage and

46
00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:16.520
thus more risk that
is available with

47
00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.280
stocks given identical
investments.

48
00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.070
Profits and losses are magnified.

49
00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:25.280
However, option holders,
unlike shareholders,

50
00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.055
do not receive the underlying
companies dividends

51
00:02:28.055 --> 00:02:29.915
or voting rights.

52
00:02:29.915 --> 00:02:33.950
For example, assume an
investor buys 1,000 shares of

53
00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:36.770
Company XYZ Corp at $10 each

54
00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:40.555
for an initial capital
outlay of $10,000.

55
00:02:40.555 --> 00:02:43.640
If the share price
increase to $11,

56
00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.530
the investors capital
would increase to

57
00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:48.865
$11,000 for a 10 percent gain.

58
00:02:48.865 --> 00:02:51.135
With option contracts at

59
00:02:51.135 --> 00:02:54.035
10 percent move in the value
of the underlying asset

60
00:02:54.035 --> 00:02:57.005
could translates into
a larger increase

61
00:02:57.005 --> 00:02:59.705
or decrease in the
price of the option.

62
00:02:59.705 --> 00:03:03.050
In the example, a
premium of $0.50 is

63
00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:07.805
paid for a call option with
a strike price of $10.25.

64
00:03:07.805 --> 00:03:09.890
If the market value of

65
00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:13.100
the underlying asset
price rises to $11,

66
00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.690
the option value rises to

67
00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:19.300
$0.75 and can be sold for
an increase of 50 percent.

68
00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.550
Option premiums and the
subsequent changes in

69
00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:25.625
option values can be predicted
through a pricing model,

70
00:03:25.625 --> 00:03:27.670
such as Black Shoals.

71
00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:30.844
Because we don't want to
overwhelm you with formulas,

72
00:03:30.844 --> 00:03:32.630
we will simplify things by taking

73
00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:34.430
you through the basic inputs of

74
00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:36.950
an option pricing model
and let you see how

75
00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:40.160
each input impacts
an option's value.

76
00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:41.810
Standard inputs of

77
00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.990
an option pricing model
are, strike price,

78
00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:48.050
interest rate,
implied volatility,

79
00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:52.445
underlying asset price,
and time to expiration.

80
00:03:52.445 --> 00:03:55.910
The strike price represents
the predetermined price

81
00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:59.030
at which an option
contract may be exercised,

82
00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:00.950
where a call option buyer can buy

83
00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:03.185
the underlying asset
from an option seller

84
00:04:03.185 --> 00:04:05.540
or a put option buyer can sell

85
00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:08.720
the underlying asset
to an option seller.

86
00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:14.200
Consider a company whose
share price is $32.90.

87
00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.160
An investor who expects

88
00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:18.380
the share price to
rise is considering

89
00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:23.735
buying a call option at
strike prices of $33 or $35.

90
00:04:23.735 --> 00:04:26.360
The investor expects
the share price to

91
00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:29.330
rise within the
following 90 days.

92
00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:31.730
The question the
investor faces is,

93
00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:33.260
what price should be paid for

94
00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:35.995
the rights conveyed
by the call option.

95
00:04:35.995 --> 00:04:38.900
The probability of the
share price rising to

96
00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.330
the $33 strike is greater

97
00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:45.215
than the probability of
the shares rising to $35.

98
00:04:45.215 --> 00:04:47.600
Therefore, the premium costs for

99
00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.210
the $33 strike option
would be higher

100
00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:53.595
than the premium cost of
the $35 strike option.

101
00:04:53.595 --> 00:04:55.460
The opposite is true for

102
00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:58.745
put options since they
convey selling rates.

103
00:04:58.745 --> 00:05:01.370
In this case, the
price of a put option

104
00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:03.530
at the $28 strike would be more

105
00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:06.050
costly than a put option
offering the right to

106
00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:09.320
sell at a lower $25 strike.

107
00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:11.780
The rate of interest
is a factor in

108
00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:14.105
determining the premium
cost of an option.

109
00:05:14.105 --> 00:05:16.610
That's because investors
are faced with

110
00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:19.445
an allocation choice
between various assets,

111
00:05:19.445 --> 00:05:21.500
including the risk-free rates of

112
00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:24.095
interest on government
securities.

113
00:05:24.095 --> 00:05:27.770
Investors tend to set the
value in the calculator at

114
00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:28.910
the rates available on

115
00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:32.225
government bills for the
period of the option.

116
00:05:32.225 --> 00:05:35.015
While the rate of
interest is a factor,

117
00:05:35.015 --> 00:05:38.855
it's significance in the
current market is fairly low.

118
00:05:38.855 --> 00:05:41.210
Having already noted
the importance

119
00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:42.470
of the relationship between

120
00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:45.935
underlying assets and strike
price to an option's price,

121
00:05:45.935 --> 00:05:48.200
It would stand to reason
that the outlook on

122
00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.810
future underlying
asset price movements

123
00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:53.030
would affect the
price of an option.

124
00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:56.345
The greater the forecasted
price movements

125
00:05:56.345 --> 00:05:58.580
or implied volatility,

126
00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:00.440
the greater the probability that

127
00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:04.040
an option will increase
or decrease in value.

128
00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:07.490
Therefore, the greater
the implied volatility,

129
00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:09.020
the greater the potential for

130
00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:11.300
the option's price to change.

131
00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:13.580
Implied volatility does not

132
00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:16.340
suggest whether prices
will go up or down,

133
00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:18.830
only that prices will move.

134
00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:22.460
Implied volatility or
a future outlook on

135
00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:24.260
price movements should not be

136
00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:26.900
confused with
historic volatility.

137
00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:29.120
While historic volatility may be

138
00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.315
a guide to determining
an implied volatility,

139
00:06:32.315 --> 00:06:35.900
an options pricing model
requires an estimate of

140
00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:40.005
implied volatility during
the option contract period.

141
00:06:40.005 --> 00:06:43.855
Option market participants
know the underlying price,

142
00:06:43.855 --> 00:06:46.525
strike price, interest rates,

143
00:06:46.525 --> 00:06:49.110
and number of days to expiration.

144
00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:51.620
Current option prices are

145
00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:53.690
readily available from
the exchanges and

146
00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:55.640
therefore implied
volatility can be

147
00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:58.820
backed into by an
options pricing model.

148
00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:01.910
Knowing implied volatility
across a range of

149
00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:04.955
different underlying
assets, strike prices,

150
00:07:04.955 --> 00:07:06.920
call options and put options

151
00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:09.200
allows a trader to easily compare

152
00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.360
options and to determine if

153
00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:14.450
price opportunities
exist in the market.

154
00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:17.450
The relationship between
the strike price and

155
00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:18.980
the underlying asset will have

156
00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:21.935
a strong influence on
the price of an option.

157
00:07:21.935 --> 00:07:23.675
For a call option,

158
00:07:23.675 --> 00:07:26.030
the higher the
underlying asset price

159
00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:28.085
in relation to the strike price,

160
00:07:28.085 --> 00:07:30.260
the higher the option premium.

161
00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:33.770
The payout at exercise of
the option is the difference

162
00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:38.570
between the underlying asset
price and the strike price.

163
00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:41.510
Options with an
underlying asset price

164
00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:44.120
below the strike are
out of the money,

165
00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:46.445
at the strike are at the money,

166
00:07:46.445 --> 00:07:50.165
and above the strike
are in the money.

167
00:07:50.165 --> 00:07:51.905
For a put option,

168
00:07:51.905 --> 00:07:54.140
the lower the
underlying asset price

169
00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.195
in relation to the strike price,

170
00:07:56.195 --> 00:07:58.385
the higher the option premium.

171
00:07:58.385 --> 00:08:00.380
The payout to the exercise

172
00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:01.850
of the option is the difference

173
00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:06.090
between underlying asset
price and the strike price.

174
00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:08.920
Options with an
underlying asset price

175
00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:11.320
above the strike price are out of

176
00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:13.840
the money at the strike at

177
00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:18.045
the money and below the
strike in the money.

178
00:08:18.045 --> 00:08:21.965
Remember that even though the
option may be in the money,

179
00:08:21.965 --> 00:08:24.620
it may still result in
a loss when considering

180
00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:28.355
both the premium and commission
paid for the option.

181
00:08:28.355 --> 00:08:30.230
While we are not factoring

182
00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:32.825
these costs in for the
sake of simplicity,

183
00:08:32.825 --> 00:08:34.775
they should be considered
when determining

184
00:08:34.775 --> 00:08:37.730
the break-even point for
the option contract.

185
00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:40.670
An option expiring today
would be worth the

186
00:08:40.670 --> 00:08:43.505
amount by which it
is in the money.

187
00:08:43.505 --> 00:08:45.380
If it is at the money,

188
00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:46.595
or out the money,

189
00:08:46.595 --> 00:08:49.970
then it has no current
intrinsic value.

190
00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:52.730
An option that has time before

191
00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:56.045
expiration generally
has extrinsic value.

192
00:08:56.045 --> 00:09:00.110
The extrinsic value represents
the probability that

193
00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:02.375
an option's underlying
asset value

194
00:09:02.375 --> 00:09:03.890
will move in the future,

195
00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:06.260
giving it the
potential to produce

196
00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:09.575
an increase in intrinsic value.

197
00:09:09.575 --> 00:09:12.155
Of the more time
until expiration,

198
00:09:12.155 --> 00:09:13.970
the greater the probability that

199
00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:16.130
an underlying asset move will

200
00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:19.715
increase an option's
intrinsic value.

201
00:09:19.715 --> 00:09:22.970
Therefore, for both
puts and calls,

202
00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:25.370
the greater the time
until expiration,

203
00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:28.520
the greater the extrinsic value.

204
00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:32.585
An options premium or
market value is equal to

205
00:09:32.585 --> 00:09:37.280
its intrinsic value plus
its extrinsic value.

206
00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:39.500
At option expiration, there is

207
00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:41.990
no extrinsic value remaining and

208
00:09:41.990 --> 00:09:44.135
so the options
market value will be

209
00:09:44.135 --> 00:09:47.045
equal to its intrinsic value.

210
00:09:47.045 --> 00:09:49.580
Two of the factors
that could affect

211
00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:54.245
an options price are option
style and dividends.

212
00:09:54.245 --> 00:09:58.550
Options style determines when
an option can be exercised.

213
00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:00.755
American style options can be

214
00:10:00.755 --> 00:10:04.490
exercised at any time
prior to, up to,

215
00:10:04.490 --> 00:10:06.470
and including the
day of expiration,

216
00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:08.555
while European style options can

217
00:10:08.555 --> 00:10:11.495
only be exercised at expiration.

218
00:10:11.495 --> 00:10:13.580
All models in these lessons

219
00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:15.575
assume American style options,

220
00:10:15.575 --> 00:10:18.815
which is the most common
type of US option.

221
00:10:18.815 --> 00:10:22.220
Dividends tend to affect
an option's price most

222
00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:25.070
when there are changes in
the dividend amount and

223
00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:26.870
around ex-dividend date due

224
00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:28.640
to the value of the dividend upon

225
00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:32.795
exercise up until the
ex-dividend date.

226
00:10:32.795 --> 00:10:35.630
Now that we have covered
the option pricing model,

227
00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:38.345
you should have a better
understanding of how changes in

228
00:10:38.345 --> 00:10:41.705
all of the inputs may
affect an option's price.

229
00:10:41.705 --> 00:10:44.240
You should also
note that rarely do

230
00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:47.640
these inputs move in isolation.